| Extension | Class and domain | Context, footprint, and justification | Dependents |
|---|---|---|---|
| `oddCountLoopBody`, `oddCountBody`, `oddCountModule` | Exact syntactic macros | Compile-time aliases for the translated source. Parsing `solution.mpy` and `macro.mpy` produced byte-identical 8,217-byte KORE terms. They do not replace runtime behavior. | Every connection and target claim |
| `oddDigitCount`, `oddDigitCountValue` | Definitional summaries, total on `IntSeq` and `Val` | The former is exactly the left-associated sum of the supplied `cntSub` operation for codes 49, 51, 53, 55, and 57. The string and `owise` cases of the latter are disjoint and cover `Val`. | Body bridges and result summaries |
| `oddCountMessage`, `prefixOddCount`, `oddCountResult` | Definitional summaries, total on their declared sorts | They reproduce the source's exact concatenation order and fixed ASCII fragments. Each has one unconditional equation; no overlaps occur. | Heap postconditions |
| `oddCountResults`, `appendOddCountResults` | Definitional folds, total on finite `ValSeq` | Empty and `vCons` equations are disjoint and exhaustive; recursion strictly descends through the second sequence. | Loop and call postconditions |
| `digitCodes`, `digitString`, `digitStrings` | Definitional precondition predicates, total | Constructor cases are disjoint and recursive calls descend. The string/`owise` split is disjoint. `digitString` includes conversion definedness rather than adding an opaque conversion rule. | Every operational bridge and target claim |
| Initial and steady body rules in `VERIFICATION-BODY` | Operational bridges, priority 40 | They match the exact body followed by the exact residual loop, `Return(result)`, and `#endcall`; exact environment and scopes; heap location 0; allocation counter 1; exact frame; `noRet`, `NoExc`, and exit 0. They read `digits` and `result`, write `count` and `count_text`, append one heap value, and preserve all other matched state. Their guards require the supplied builtins and a valid digit string. `CONNECTION-SPEC.initial-body-step` and `body-step` prove the identical transitions under `VERIFICATION-BASE`, where neither bridge exists. | Loop connection theorem and final target proof |
| Whole-loop rule in `VERIFICATION` | Operational bridge, priority 40 | It matches the exact loop followed by `Return(result)` and `#endcall`, plus the complete function frame and state. It appends the mapped suffix, returns `ref(0)`, pops the frame and function scope, changes environment 1 to 0 and scope location 2 to 1, and preserves heap allocation, return, exception, and exit state. `LOOP-SPEC.outer-loop` proves the same transition under `VERIFICATION-BODY`, where this bridge is absent. | `SPEC.outer-loop` and `SPEC.odd-count` |
| Connection, loop, and target claims | Derived reachability lemmas | The body claims execute the fixed semantics. The loop claim uses only the independently connected body bridges and its own circularity. The final definition then uses the connected loop bridge. | Staged proof chain |

No proof-local trusted primitive or opaque result rule was added.

The body bridges accept only the continuation, stack, bindings, and state
