+See `evidence/32-krun-concrete-harness.log`.
+
+## 4. Adequacy and real-program pinning
+
+### Plain-language claims and satisfiable preconditions
+
+| Claim | Precondition and realizable witness | Postcondition |
+|---|---|---|
+| Initial body connection | Exact function frame with `lst`, `result`, and a current digit string but no previous `count` fields; the builtins scope is exact and `digitString(str(CS))` holds. Witness: `CS = "1"`, empty accumulator and remaining suffix. | Executes the submitted loop body once, binds count/text, appends one formatted value, and resumes the exact residual loop/return/endcall continuation. |
+| Steady body connection | Same exact context, now with old `count` and `count_text`. Witness: `CS = "1"`, old values zero/empty. | Overwrites those two fields, appends one result, and resumes the exact continuation. |
+| Loop connection and `SPEC.outer-loop` | Exact active function frame with an accumulated heap list and a remaining suffix `VS` satisfying `digitStrings(VS)`. Witness: `VS = .ValSeq`, empty accumulator, arbitrary concrete old fields. | Consumes the loop and following return/endcall, returns `ref(0)`, appends `oddCountResults(VS)`, deletes the callee scope, restores environment/allocation location, and pops the exact frame. |
+| `SPEC.odd-count` | Exact initial module configuration and a finite `ValSeq` of strings whose codes are 48 through 57, with integer-to-decimal conversion defined. Witnesses: `VS = .ValSeq` and the encoding of `["1234567"]`. | Returns `ref(0)`; heap location zero contains exactly `list(oddCountResults(VS))`; the only remaining user binding is the actual `odd_count` closure; allocation, stack, return, exception, and exit cells are fixed. |
+| Two continuation claims | A concrete `"1"` body state followed by a distinct sentinel assignment. | The body executes normally and the sentinel remains observable, both with and without bridge modules. |
+
+All preconditions are conjunctions over concrete algebraic K constructors and
+are satisfiable; none contains a contradiction. In particular,
+`digitStrings(.ValSeq)` rewrites to `true`, and the documented example's digit
+codes satisfy the digit predicate.
+
+### Pinning the actual submitted program
+
+The target `<k>` starts with `#loadAll(oddCountModule)` and then performs a
+normal name-based `Call`. The three `oddCount*` names are declared compile-time
+macros. I independently parsed both the submitted `solution.mpy` and an
+auditor-authored one-line `oddCountModule` term using the clean
+`VERIFICATION-BASE` definition:
+
+```text
+kast solution.mpy --definition fresh-connection-kompiled --input program --output kore
+kast auditor-macro.mpy --definition fresh-connection-kompiled --input program --output kore
+cmp -s auditor-solution.kore auditor-macro.kore
+```
+
+Both KORE files are 8,217 bytes, have SHA-256
+`50a6026af553a150382a028c2a8e588d57a4a5d499d54e2e9fe32a7a2264d574`,
+and compare identical. See `evidence/19-program-macro-identity.log`. The proof
